/* Original lessons organized around the user's supplied 24-lesson curriculum. */
const modules = [
 'Professional Firmware Debugging Methodology',
 'Arm Cortex-M Debug Architecture and Tools',
 'HardFaults, Exceptions and Memory Corruption',
 'Interrupts, Timing and Peripheral Debugging',
 'Firmware Testing, Regression and Professional Reporting'
];
const freertosModules = [
 'Task Scheduling and Context Switching',
 'Priority, Preemption and Priority Inversion',
 'Queues, Semaphores and ISR Safety',
 'Memory, Stack and Static Allocation',
 'Task Notifications, Timers and Idle Power'
];
const lessons = [];
const freertosLessons = [];
function lesson(module,title,concept,example,code,steps,expected,exercise,question,answer,h7='Repeat on your exact H7 part. Record the selected core, clock tree and memory region; do not copy F401 peripheral addresses or clock settings.') {
 lessons.push({id:lessons.length+1,module,title,concept,example,code,steps,expected,exercise,question,answer,h7});
}
function rtosLesson(module,title,concept,example,code,steps,expected,exercise,question,answer,h7='Repeat on your exact H7 part. Record the selected core, clock tree and memory region; do not copy F401 peripheral addresses or clock settings.') {
 freertosLessons.push({id:freertosLessons.length+1,module,title,concept,example,code,steps,expected,exercise,question,answer,h7});
 freertosLessons[freertosLessons.length-1].position = freertosLessons.length;
}
lesson(0,'Debugging as an Engineering Investigation',
 'Debugging connects an observed failure to a mechanism that explains it. A symptom is what happened; a hypothesis predicts why; an experiment distinguishes explanations. A correction is credible only when the original failure disappears and a regression test demonstrates why. Think of a doctor measuring temperature before choosing treatment: changing five settings at once destroys the evidence.',
 'A sensor gateway stops printing after a burst of UART traffic. Possible explanations include a full receive buffer, an unserviced interrupt, or blocking work in an ISR. Start by recording the last successful sequence number and whether a GPIO heartbeat still runs. A live heartbeat narrows the investigation; it does not prove the UART hardware is healthy.',
 '/* Add to a Cube-generated main.c; no UART needed yet. */\nvolatile uint32_t heartbeat = 0;\nvolatile uint32_t checkpoint = 0;\n/* In the existing while (1) loop: */\ncheckpoint = 1;\nheartbeat++;\nHAL_Delay(100);\ncheckpoint = 2;',
 ['Create a board-selected STM32CubeIDE project with Serial Wire debug enabled. Build with debug symbols and -Og.','Connect ST-LINK, flash, and break at main. Add heartbeat and checkpoint to Expressions.','Resume for two seconds, halt, and record values, PC, build commit and reset type.','Write three hypotheses for an apparent stopped heartbeat. Choose one observation that would distinguish each.'],
 'The counter advances when running and stops when the CPU is halted. A debugger pause is an intervention, not normal runtime behavior.',
 'Capture an evidence sheet before modifying code. Repeat ten runs and distinguish a stopped CPU from a stopped application loop.',
 'What is the difference between a workaround and a root-cause fix?',
 'A workaround avoids the trigger; a root-cause fix corrects the causal mechanism. Increasing a buffer may postpone an overflow. Correcting capacity checks and proving bounded producer/consumer behavior addresses it.');
lesson(0,'Reproducing Firmware Failures',
 'A reproducible failure has controlled inputs and a measurable pass/fail rule. Preserve the binary, configuration and stimulus. A failure rate needs a denominator: “failed three times” means little without the number of attempts. Cold boot, warm reset and debugger reset can leave different state.',
 'A packet parser fails on the 65th byte of a 64-byte buffer. Use a fixed byte pattern and test lengths 0, 1, 63, 64 and 65. Log the accepted length and rejection count, not just a message saying that something went wrong.',
 '#include <stdbool.h>\n#include <stdint.h>\n#include <stddef.h>\nstatic uint8_t packet[64];\nstatic size_t used;\nstatic uint32_t rejected;\nbool append_byte(uint8_t value) {\n    if (used >= sizeof packet) { rejected++; return false; }\n    packet[used++] = value;\n    return true;\n}',
 ['Add append_byte to a scratch project; set used and rejected to zero before each run.','Call it with exactly 63, 64 and 65 bytes using a deterministic loop.','Watch used and rejected, and record expected versus actual values.','Repeat after power cycling and after debugger reset. Save the ELF and compiler settings.'],
 '64 bytes are accepted; the 65th is rejected. No write beyond packet occurs.',
 'Create a 100-run matrix with stimulus length, reset mode, build ID and result. Change only one variable per comparison.',
 'How do you investigate a failure that occurs only once a day?',
 'Add bounded telemetry and a retained crash record, preserve environmental data, then accelerate the suspected trigger without changing the mechanism. Report failure rate and observation time; absence of failure is not proof of impossibility.');
lesson(0,'Hypothesis-Driven Fault Isolation',
 'A useful hypothesis makes a falsifiable prediction. Divide a path into stages and observe the boundaries: electrical signal → peripheral → DMA or ISR → buffer → parser → application. Check the earliest divergence from expected behavior. Binary isolation works when a subsystem can be replaced with a controlled input.',
 'A temperature reading is stale. If a synthetic sequence reaches the display but real readings do not, the application output is less likely to be the cause. Inspect peripheral completion and buffer ownership next. Replacing the sensor and rewriting the parser simultaneously would lose this distinction.',
 '/* Supply this in place of the sensor read during isolation. */\nstatic uint32_t sequence;\nuint32_t synthetic_sample(void) {\n    return sequence++;\n}',
 ['Write the complete sample-to-output path. Add a counter at each stage.','Substitute synthetic_sample at the application boundary.','Move the substitution upstream one stage at a time.','For each run, write a prediction before running it, then record whether it survived.'],
 'A table identifies the first stage where the measured sequence differs from the injected sequence.',
 'Investigate a missing UART message using three competing hypotheses and two experiments that distinguish them.',
 'Does a failure disappearing with logging prove a timing bug?',
 'It is evidence that instrumentation changes behavior, but not proof of a specific cause. Logging can change timing, memory layout, stack depth and compiler optimization. Use lower-impact observations and test individual predictions.');
lesson(0,'Root-Cause Analysis and Evidence',
 'Root cause is a mechanism supported by a causal chain: trigger, defective operation, corrupted state and visible failure. Separate direct observations from inference. A strong report includes an experiment that reproduces the defect, a minimal correction and a test that fails before the correction and passes after it.',
 'At length 64, code writes a string terminator to buffer[64] even though the final valid index is 63. The adjacent length field changes. The mechanism is an out-of-bounds terminator write; the visible symptom may be a parser timeout much later.',
 '#include <stddef.h>\n#include <stdbool.h>\nbool terminate(char *buf, size_t capacity, size_t length) {\n    if (buf == NULL || length >= capacity) return false;\n    buf[length] = \'\\0\';\n    return true;\n}',
 ['Draw the buffer and adjacent fields using addresses from the debugger.','Inspect the suspect write in disassembly without deliberately executing an out-of-range C write.','Apply the checked terminator and test lengths capacity-1 and capacity.','Document the chain and attach pre-fix evidence, corrected code and test results.'],
 'Length equal to capacity is rejected; the final valid terminator position is capacity-1.',
 'Write a one-page report containing symptom, trigger, evidence, mechanism, correction, regression and remaining uncertainty.',
 'Why is “it works after adding a delay” insufficient?',
 'A delay changes scheduling and may mask missing synchronization. Explain which invariant was violated and demonstrate it with observations. Correct the ownership or completion protocol rather than relying on incidental timing.');
lesson(1,'Cortex-M4 Debug Architecture',
 'SWD connects a probe to the debug access port, allowing the debugger to control execution and inspect memory. The Cortex-M debug components provide halt control, flash breakpoint comparators, data watchpoints and optional trace. The CPU, probe and board wiring each constrain which features are usable. Debug access and application UART are separate paths.',
 'On a NUCLEO-F401RE, the onboard ST-LINK provides a convenient SWD connection. A standalone F401 board needs a compatible external probe. A working USB serial port does not prove that SWDIO, SWCLK, ground and target voltage reference are connected.',
 '/* GDB commands after connecting through the IDE debug server */\nmonitor reset halt\ninfo registers\nx/4wx 0xE000ED00\n/* SCB identification/status area; read only here. */',
 ['Identify the exact board and MCU marking; open its schematic and reference manual.','Verify target power, shared ground, SWDIO, SWCLK and reset routing; do not connect two conflicting power sources.','Start an ST-LINK debug session and halt at main.','Inspect PC, SP and xPSR, then compare PC with the ELF symbol for main.'],
 'The debugger can halt, read core state and locate main in the loaded ELF.',
 'Draw host → GDB server → probe → SWD → core/memory. Annotate which link you would check for “no target found”.',
 'Can SWD always inspect a running target without changing behavior?',
 'No. Memory access can contend for buses, reads of some peripheral registers have side effects, and halt-based views stop execution. Trace can reduce intrusion, but support and bandwidth are limited.',
 'For an H743 use the Cortex-M7 core documentation. For dual-core H745/H747 boards select the correct core and understand reset ownership; another core or DMA can continue modifying shared state.');
lesson(1,'Breakpoints, Stepping and Execution Control',
 'A breakpoint halts at an instruction; source lines can map to several instructions or none. Step into follows calls, step over temporarily runs through a call, and step out runs to a return. Hardware breakpoints in flash are finite. Conditional breakpoints may repeatedly halt and evaluate conditions on the host, affecting timing.',
 'HAL_Delay depends on a tick interrupt in a typical HAL project. Stepping through it can look like a hang because the tick and CPU are not advancing as they would during normal execution. Use run-to-line beyond the call and inspect the tick configuration if it never completes while freely running.',
 'volatile uint32_t iteration;\nvoid debug_target(void) {\n    for (iteration = 0; iteration < 10; ++iteration) {\n        __NOP(); /* Set breakpoint here; inspect iteration. */\n    }\n}',
 ['Call debug_target after initialization. Set a breakpoint inside its loop.','Compare step into, step over and resume. Record PC and iteration.','Set a conditional breakpoint for iteration == 7; observe the number of stops.','Remove breakpoints and repeat at full speed. Check watchdog/timer debug-freeze settings before assuming they pause.'],
 'The conditional stop occurs at iteration 7; freely running execution completes all ten iterations.',
 'Locate the flash hardware breakpoint limit reported by your debugger and explain why a timing investigation should avoid frequent halts.',
 'Why does source stepping jump between lines?',
 'Optimizations can reorder, inline or remove operations; debug line information maps instructions back imperfectly. Inspect disassembly and use the exact ELF rather than assuming one source line equals one instruction.');
lesson(1,'Watchpoints and Runtime State Inspection',
 'A watchpoint traps a CPU access to a chosen address using a data comparator. It is ideal for finding who overwrites a variable. Watch length, alignment, access type and available comparators matter. A debugger memory window samples state; it is not a timestamped history. DMA writes may not trigger a core data watchpoint.',
 'A configuration value changes unexpectedly. Watch the address of the value for writes, resume, and inspect the stopped instruction and call stack. If corruption comes from DMA, investigate DMA addresses and transfer counts instead of concluding that no writer exists.',
 'volatile uint32_t configuration = 115200;\nvoid change_configuration(void) {\n    configuration = 9600;\n}\n/* GDB: watch configuration\n   Continue, then inspect bt and x/i $pc. */',
 ['Add the function and call it once from the main loop.','Set a write watchpoint on configuration after initialization.','Resume and inspect the instruction around PC; a trap can be reported after the access.','Remove the watchpoint, use a memory view, and explain why the view alone cannot identify the writer.'],
 'A CPU write stops execution near change_configuration; the stack identifies the calling path.',
 'Place two neighboring aligned variables in memory, watch one, and verify the watched byte range before interpreting a stop.',
 'Why might a variable change without a watchpoint firing?',
 'Possible causes include DMA or another bus master, another core, an incorrect watched address or size, a disabled comparator, or a variable moving under optimization. Verify each before drawing a conclusion.');
lesson(1,'Disassembly, ELF Files and Linker Maps',
 'The ELF contains sections, symbols and optional debug information. A linker map explains placement and size. Disassembly shows actual instructions. A raw binary does not retain the same symbolic context. Always pair a crash address with the exact ELF and load address from the failing build.',
 'A crash report contains PC 0x08001234. Resolve it against the matching ELF, inspect the surrounding instruction and determine the address used by that instruction. Resolving it against yesterday’s build can confidently point at the wrong function.',
 'arm-none-eabi-size Debug/app.elf\narm-none-eabi-nm -n Debug/app.elf\narm-none-eabi-objdump -d -S Debug/app.elf > app.dis\narm-none-eabi-addr2line -e Debug/app.elf -f -C 0x08001234\n# Replace app.elf and PC with your actual artifact and crash PC.',
 ['Build and locate the ELF and map; enable linker map output if absent.','Find main, .text, .data and .bss in the map.','Resolve the address of your own debug_target function with addr2line.','Inspect one load/store instruction and calculate its effective address from registers.'],
 'Your function address maps to its actual source; initialized data has a RAM runtime location and a flash load image.',
 'Archive an ELF, map and compiler flags under one build ID. Explain how a bootloader offset changes address interpretation.',
 'What is the difference between .data and .bss?',
 '.data holds initialized writable objects; startup copies initial values from the load image into RAM. .bss holds zero-initialized storage; startup clears it. Neither describes stack usage.',
 'Inspect the H7 linker script for AXI SRAM, DTCM and other RAM regions. A symbol being in RAM does not establish DMA reachability for a given DMA controller.');
lesson(1,'Debug Versus Release Firmware',
 'Debug and release are build configurations, not processor modes. Optimization, assertions, logging and link-time optimization can differ. Undefined behavior may surface differently as register allocation or layout changes. volatile requires observable accesses but does not fix data races or make compound operations atomic.',
 'A main loop waiting for an ISR-set flag can fail under optimization when the object is not declared for asynchronous observation. Even after volatile is added, a read-modify-write counter shared between contexts can still lose updates.',
 'static volatile uint32_t ready;\n/* In an enabled interrupt handler or HAL callback: */\nvoid signal_ready(void) { ready = 1U; }\n/* In main: this is a one-shot flag, not an event queue. */\nvoid wait_ready(void) {\n    while (ready == 0U) { __NOP(); }\n}',
 ['Build the same one-shot flag example with -Og and -O2 plus -g.','Compare disassembly of the polling loop and symbol availability.','Keep the interrupt producer enabled and verify it actually runs.','Record all configuration differences before diagnosing an optimization issue.'],
 'The polling load remains observable. Multiple events are not counted by this one-bit protocol.',
 'List three sources of undefined behavior and use compiler warnings to review parser code for them.',
 'Would you ship a debug build to solve a release-only crash?',
 'Only as a temporary containment decision with measured impact. Investigate undefined behavior, concurrency, stack use and configuration differences using a symbolized optimized build, then validate the production configuration.');
lesson(2,'Cortex-M Exception and Fault Architecture',
 'Exceptions redirect execution to a handler through the vector table. Configurable faults include MemManage, BusFault and UsageFault; HardFault can result when a fault escalates. A reset loop, a lockup and a HardFault are distinct observations. Enable configurable handlers deliberately and inspect their status instead of labeling every stop a HardFault.',
 'An integer divide by zero is not guaranteed to trap unless the trap control is enabled. Use a deliberate UDF instruction for a controlled UsageFault experiment, first with the configurable handler disabled, then enabled. This avoids relying on undefined C arithmetic.',
 '/* CMSIS, GNU Arm; run only in the isolated fault lab. */\nvoid inject_usage_fault(void) {\n    __asm volatile ("udf #0");\n}\n/* Optional: enable UsageFault before injection. */\n/* SCB->SHCSR |= SCB_SHCSR_USGFAULTENA_Msk; */',
 ['Work in a disposable firmware project, with a debugger attached.','Set breakpoints in HardFault_Handler and UsageFault_Handler.','With UsageFault disabled, inject UDF and inspect HFSR and CFSR.','Reset, enable UsageFault, repeat, and compare the entered handler.'],
 'An undefined instruction sets the corresponding UsageFault status; a disabled configurable fault normally escalates to HardFault.',
 'Create an exception table showing reset, NMI, HardFault, configurable faults, SysTick and external interrupts.',
 'What does HFSR.FORCED tell you?',
 'A configurable-priority fault escalated to HardFault. It is not the original cause; inspect CFSR and relevant context to identify the underlying fault.');
lesson(2,'Exception Entry and Stacked Register Context',
 'On normal exception entry the basic frame contains R0, R1, R2, R3, R12, LR, PC and xPSR. The handler LR contains EXC_RETURN, which describes return context including the selected stack. An extended floating-point frame and stack alignment can change interpretation. If stacking itself failed, the assumed frame may be invalid.',
 'A task running with PSP faults, but the handler uses MSP. Reading MSP as the interrupted task frame gives misleading values. Check EXC_RETURN bit 2 to select the interrupted stack and bit 4 for basic versus extended frame before decoding.',
 '/* GNU Arm, basic-frame teaching example only. */\n__attribute__((naked)) void HardFault_Handler(void) {\n    __asm volatile (\n        "tst lr, #4\\n"\n        "ite eq\\n"\n        "mrseq r0, msp\\n"\n        "mrsne r0, psp\\n"\n        "mov r1, lr\\n"\n        "b capture_fault\\n");\n}\n/* capture_fault(raw_sp, exc_return) must validate RAM bounds,\n   stacking fault bits and frame type before dereferencing.\n   Do not install this without implementing that function. */',
 ['First use a breakpoint in the existing generated fault handler; no handler replacement is required.','Read handler LR and both MSP and PSP in the register view.','For a valid basic frame, examine eight words at the selected stack address.','Compare stacked PC with the injected UDF instruction; inspect xPSR and stack alignment.'],
 'The valid stacked PC identifies the interrupted instruction stream; live handler PC identifies the fault handler.',
 'Sketch a basic frame, then explain why a floating-point extended frame requires a different core-register offset.',
 'Why should a fault handler avoid printf and heap allocation?',
 'The stack, heap, library locks or peripheral state may already be compromised. Record a minimal bounded snapshot using a validated path; avoid recursive faults and defer formatting until after recovery.');
lesson(2,'Fault Status Registers',
 'CFSR combines memory-management, bus and usage fault status. HFSR helps explain escalation. MMFAR and BFAR are meaningful only when their validity bits are set. Some status bits are sticky and write-one-to-clear: capture them before clearing. An imprecise bus fault may be reported after the initiating instruction.',
 'A BusFault flag with BFARVALID clear does not justify treating BFAR as the offending address. Save the raw status and decode only fields that are valid. In a stacking error, be cautious about both the frame and the reported PC.',
 '/* Inspect while halted; reading SCB status does not clear it. */\nuint32_t cfsr = SCB->CFSR;\nuint32_t hfsr = SCB->HFSR;\nuint32_t bfar = SCB->BFAR;\nuint32_t mmfar = SCB->MMFAR;\n/* To clear captured W1C flags deliberately:\n   SCB->CFSR = cfsr;  // write captured 1 bits, not an RMW */',
 ['Trigger the controlled UDF lab and halt in the fault handler.','Save raw CFSR, HFSR, SHCSR, BFAR and MMFAR values.','Decode each set bit using the core programming manual.','Record whether address-valid and stacking-error bits allow reliable interpretation.'],
 'UNDEFINSTR is visible in CFSR for the UDF example. Stale address registers are not presented as evidence.',
 'Decode a synthetic CFSR value 0x00008200: identify PRECISERR and BFARVALID and state what additional evidence is needed.',
 'Can the stacked PC always locate the bad memory access?',
 'No. Precise faults often identify it, but buffered writes, imprecise faults and failed exception stacking can invalidate that assumption. Correlate fault type, instruction stream and memory transaction context.');
lesson(2,'Finding the Faulting Instruction',
 'Move from fault classification to instruction-level evidence. Resolve the valid stacked PC against the exact ELF, disassemble nearby instructions, inspect operand registers and calculate the effective address. A backtrace is helpful but can be wrong after stack corruption or without correct unwind information.',
 'A load instruction uses R3 plus an offset. If R3 is a corrupted pointer, calculate the requested address and compare it with the device memory map. The crash line can be a consequence of an earlier overwrite, so finding the instruction is the beginning of causal analysis.',
 '# In the GDB console, substitute a validated stacked PC:\ninfo line *0x08001234\nx/8i 0x08001228\ninfo registers\nbt\n# Offline: arm-none-eabi-addr2line -e app.elf -f -C <PC>\n# Live handler registers are not all interrupted registers.',
 ['Reuse the deterministic UDF fault and archive its ELF.','Locate the actual stacked PC and inspect its instruction.','Compare stacked R0–R3 with any operands used; remember R4–R11 are not in the basic frame.','Write what is observed and what remains unknown about the caller.'],
 'The PC resolves to the deliberate undefined instruction rather than to HardFault_Handler.',
 'Given LDR R2,[R3,#8] and R3=0x20000100, calculate the effective address, then verify it against your exact part memory map.',
 'How can a valid backtrace still mislead?',
 'It may identify where corrupted data was consumed rather than where it was corrupted. Investigate object lifetime, ownership and earlier writers; use watchpoints or bounded trace to find the first violation.');
lesson(2,'Common Memory Corruption Defects',
 'Out-of-bounds writes, dangling pointers, incorrect lengths and format mismatches can corrupt unrelated state. The observed crash may occur long after the defect. Prevent invalid accesses using capacity-aware interfaces and test boundaries. Canaries detect some corruption but do not make unsafe writes acceptable.',
 'A length field arrives from a UART packet. Validate the claimed payload length against both the received byte count and destination capacity before copying. A valid destination alone does not prove the source contains enough bytes.',
 '#include <stdint.h>\n#include <stddef.h>\n#include <stdbool.h>\n#include <string.h>\nbool copy_payload(uint8_t *dst, size_t cap,\n                  const uint8_t *src, size_t available, size_t n) {\n    if (!dst || !src || n > cap || n > available) return false;\n    /* Contract: src and dst do not overlap. */\n    memcpy(dst, src, n);\n    return true;\n}',
 ['Create source and destination arrays with known patterns.','Test zero length with valid pointers, exact capacity, and capacity+1.','Check insufficient source length separately from insufficient destination space.','Inspect surrounding memory without executing intentional undefined writes.'],
 'Invalid lengths are rejected before copying; valid lengths preserve expected bytes.',
 'Extend the API to return an error reason and write a test for each rejection path. Define behavior for overlapping buffers.',
 'Why can adding a local variable hide a crash?',
 'It can change stack layout and register allocation, moving what gets corrupted. That is a clue to investigate undefined behavior or memory corruption, not evidence that the new variable corrected the bug.');
lesson(2,'Stack and Heap Failure Analysis',
 'Each active call consumes stack for saved state and locals; nested interrupts add frames. Heap exhaustion and stack overflow have different causes. A linker-reserved stack size is not a runtime measurement. Watermarks estimate observed usage but cannot prove a worst case that has never executed.',
 'A parser with a large local array works in main but crashes when called from an interrupt. The ISR context has additional stacking and may nest. Move large persistent buffers into owned storage and bound recursion; do not simply enlarge memory without measuring.',
 '/* Enable compiler stack reports for a useful static baseline. */\n/* GCC flag: -fstack-usage */\n/* Review .su files plus the call graph and interrupt nesting. */\n/* Avoid large automatic arrays in deep or ISR call chains. */\nstatic uint8_t parser_workspace[256];\n/* Define one owner; static storage alone is not reentrant. */',
 ['Build with -fstack-usage and locate the generated .su files.','Calculate a plausible deepest call path including interrupt nesting.','Compare linker RAM usage with runtime stack and heap reservations.','If using FreeRTOS, measure each task high-water mark under stress and document that units depend on the API/port.'],
 'The report distinguishes static RAM, stack budget, observed stack use and dynamic allocation failures.',
 'Create a stack budget table for main plus two nested ISRs, including basic/FP frame assumptions and margin.',
 'What do you do when malloc returns NULL?',
 'Handle the failure without dereferencing it, preserve system invariants and use an explicit recovery policy. Investigate allocation size, fragmentation and leaks. Fixed pools or startup allocation can simplify real-time memory behavior.');
lesson(3,'Debugging Peripheral Register Configuration',
 'A peripheral needs more than its enable bit: clock gating, reset release, pin mode and alternate function, bus clock, peripheral timing and status handling must agree. Read back configuration against the exact reference manual. Some status/data reads clear flags or consume data, so an IDE peripheral view can affect behavior.',
 'A UART transmitter appears silent. Check clock enable, GPIO alternate function, selected TX pin, baud divisor and actual pin voltage with a logic analyzer. Correct register values on the wrong pin still produce no signal at your connector.',
 '/* After CubeMX has initialized your board-selected UART: */\n/* In Expressions / SFR view inspect the actual instance:\n   GPIOx->MODER, GPIOx->AFR[...], RCC clock enables,\n   UART control, baud and status registers.\n   F401 uses USART SR/DR; many H7 USARTs use ISR/RDR/TDR.\n   Do not copy register-clearing sequences between them. */',
 ['Choose a UART instance and pins using your board schematic.','Generate initialization, transmit a fixed 0x55 pattern, and record the configured clock source.','Measure bit period on TX and compare with 1/baud.','Check clock, pin and UART settings in that order, noting any side-effecting reads.'],
 'The measured bit period matches the intended baud within oscillator and measurement tolerance.',
 'Explain how a wrong peripheral kernel clock can produce a consistent but incorrect baud rate.',
 'Why can opening the peripheral register view change a bug?',
 'It may repeatedly read registers that clear status flags or consume received data. Consult access semantics and use software snapshots or trace where live reads would disturb the peripheral.',
 'H7 peripheral kernel clocks can differ from bus clocks. Verify the actual UART clock mux, GPIO alternate function and register layout in the exact H7 reference manual.');
lesson(3,'NVIC and Interrupt Faults',
 'An interrupt requires a peripheral event, peripheral interrupt enable, NVIC enable, acceptable priority/masking and the correct vector. Lower numerical priority means higher urgency on Cortex-M. Priority grouping and implemented priority bits affect preemption. A pending bit alone does not prove a handler ran.',
 'A timer flag is set but the callback never runs. Check the IRQ name in the vector table, NVIC enable, PRIMASK/BASEPRI and whether the handler calls the HAL handler for the correct timer. A handler that never clears its source can starve the main loop.',
 'volatile uint32_t timer_events;\nvoid HAL_TIM_PeriodElapsedCallback(TIM_HandleTypeDef *htim) {\n    if (htim->Instance == TIM2) {\n        timer_events++; /* Keep ISR work bounded. */\n    }\n}\n/* Configure TIM2 and enable its interrupt in CubeMX first. */',
 ['Configure a 10 Hz TIM2 update interrupt for your board clock.','Check that TIM2_IRQHandler calls HAL_TIM_IRQHandler with the correct handle.','Resume for ten seconds and measure the event count without frequent halts.','Disable and restore only the NVIC enable bit; compare peripheral flags, pending state and callback count.'],
 'About 100 events occur in ten seconds; the tolerance and measurement window are recorded.',
 'Build an interrupt diagnosis tree: no source flag, source without pending IRQ, pending without handler, handler without callback.',
 'Can a high-priority ISR call any FreeRTOS API?',
 'No. Only permitted FromISR APIs may be used, and the interrupt priority must obey the port’s syscall priority restrictions. Check implemented priority bits and shifted versus unshifted configuration values.');
lesson(3,'Shared Variables and Race Conditions',
 'Concurrency bugs arise when an operation that appears singular consists of multiple accesses. volatile does not make counter++ atomic. Protect the entire invariant, not just individual loads. A short critical section can safely snapshot and clear an ISR-updated counter on a single core; preserve the previous mask state.',
 'Main reads pending_events and then clears it. An interrupt between those operations adds an event that the clear loses. Snapshot and clear together so each event belongs to exactly one processing batch.',
 'static volatile uint32_t pending_events;\nvoid on_event_isr(void) { pending_events++; }\nuint32_t take_events(void) {\n    uint32_t previous = __get_PRIMASK();\n    __disable_irq();\n    uint32_t count = pending_events;\n    pending_events = 0;\n    __set_PRIMASK(previous);\n    return count;\n}\n/* Single core, maskable ISR producer, bounded critical section.\n   Define an overflow policy; this does not exclude DMA/NMI. */',
 ['Use a timer ISR as producer and main as consumer.','Count total produced and processed events, allowing for pending events.','Introduce a delay between the unprotected read and clear to make the original race easier to observe.','Replace with take_events and repeat under maximum intended interrupt rate.'],
 'Produced = processed + pending, subject to the explicitly defined counter overflow limit.',
 'Explain why blindly calling __enable_irq at the end can break a caller that already had interrupts disabled.',
 'When is a critical section insufficient?',
 'When the other writer is DMA, an unmasked exception or another core, or when the operation blocks too long. Use an ownership protocol, hardware semaphore, suitable atomics or RTOS primitives as appropriate.',
 'On dual-core H7, masking local interrupts does not lock the other core. Add an inter-core ownership protocol and cache-coherency design where shared RAM is cached.');
lesson(3,'Firmware Timing Analysis',
 'Measure elapsed time with a timer, cycle counter or GPIO pulse instead of inferring performance from source code. Separate execution time, interrupt latency and end-to-end deadline. DWT CYCCNT wraps at 32 bits and its availability, sleep behavior and debug configuration must be checked. Wall-clock timing under debugger halt is misleading.',
 'A control loop must finish within 100 microseconds. Measure a GPIO pulse around the work using an analyzer, then compare cycle measurements under realistic interrupt load. Report maximum observed latency and load conditions, not only the average.',
 'CoreDebug->DEMCR |= CoreDebug_DEMCR_TRCENA_Msk;\nDWT->CYCCNT = 0;\nDWT->CTRL |= DWT_CTRL_CYCCNTENA_Msk;\nuint32_t begin = DWT->CYCCNT;\n/* Work under test; retain observable results. */\n__NOP();\nuint32_t elapsed = DWT->CYCCNT - begin;\n/* Seconds = elapsed / actual core frequency.\n   Unsigned subtraction handles one wrap only if interval < 2^32 cycles. */',
 ['Enable CYCCNT and confirm it increments on your target; account for a DWT unlock requirement where applicable.','Measure an empty region to estimate instrumentation cost.','Measure real work under idle and interrupt-heavy conditions.','Compare with a GPIO pulse and document frequency, cache state and maximum observed value.'],
 'Measurements include a defined start/end, clock rate and instrumentation overhead.',
 'Calculate the 32-bit wrap interval at 84 MHz and at 400 MHz. Explain why a benchmark is not a worst-case proof.',
 'Why can two identical functions have different timings on H7?',
 'Instruction/data cache state, flash wait states, memory placement, bus contention and interrupts can change timing. Benchmark warm and cold conditions and record the memory and clock configuration.',
 'On Cortex-M7 H7 devices, compare cache-enabled warm runs with deliberate cold-start conditions. Do not generalize cycle results to every STM32H7 member or clock configuration.');
lesson(3,'UART, Buffers and DMA Failure Modes',
 'A UART DMA receiver has multiple state machines: UART, DMA, buffer ownership and parser. Track bytes produced and consumed, transfer boundaries and overrun. Circular DMA counters wrap; an idle event signals a pause, not necessarily a complete protocol packet. On cached Cortex-M7 devices, DMA and CPU may see different copies of the same RAM.',
 'A receive buffer looks stale on H743 but works on F401. Before blaming the parser, verify DMA can reach the selected RAM and inspect transfer progress. Then check cache coherency and ownership. volatile does not invalidate a data cache line.',
 '/* H743 GNU Arm example: reserve a dedicated aligned buffer.\n   Link .dma_rx into a DMA-reachable SRAM region for your DMA. */\n__attribute__((section(".dma_rx"), aligned(32)))\nstatic uint8_t rx[64];\n/* With DMA stopped and buffer exclusively owned, before RX: */\nSCB_CleanInvalidateDCache_by_Addr((uint32_t *)rx, sizeof rx);\n/* Start DMA; CPU must not write this buffer while DMA owns it.\n   After confirmed completion, before CPU consumes data: */\nSCB_InvalidateDCache_by_Addr((uint32_t *)rx, sizeof rx);\n/* Dedicated whole cache lines prevent damage to adjacent data.\n   This is not a complete circular-DMA ownership implementation. */',
 ['First implement UART reception on F401 with a bounded fixed-length buffer; test lengths 1, N-1, N and N+1.','Record UART error flags and DMA remaining count, plus parser sequence numbers.','On H743, place a dedicated aligned buffer in verified DMA-reachable RAM and inspect the map.','Define handoff/completion before cache maintenance; compare a documented non-cacheable MPU design with an explicit maintenance design.'],
 'Sequence numbers are preserved, overflow is detected, and cached H7 reads reflect completed DMA writes.',
 'Design a double-buffer ownership diagram and test back-to-back frames, partial frames, DMA wrap and delayed consumers.',
 'Why is invalidating a dirty, unaligned RX buffer dangerous?',
 'Invalidation can discard CPU modifications in neighboring cache-line data or the buffer itself. Use dedicated aligned full lines, prepare them before DMA ownership, and perform post-completion invalidation under an explicit handoff protocol.',
 'This cache lab specifically targets Cortex-M7 H743/H745-class devices. Check your exact H7 core, cache line size, DMA reachability and MPU attributes. F401 has no Cortex-M7-style L1 D-cache coherence problem.');
lesson(4,'Functional, Boundary and Negative Testing',
 'Functional tests verify intended behavior; boundary tests probe edges; negative tests verify handling of invalid inputs. Specify an oracle before the run. Include configuration, expected output and evidence. A test that only checks “did not crash” may miss silent data loss.',
 'For a packet length validator, valid lengths pass, the maximum permitted length passes, and the first excessive length is rejected. A malformed length must not partially update live application configuration.',
 '#include <assert.h>\n#include <stdbool.h>\n#include <stddef.h>\nbool valid_length(size_t n) { return n >= 1 && n <= 64; }\nvoid test_lengths(void) {\n    assert(!valid_length(0));\n    assert(valid_length(1));\n    assert(valid_length(64));\n    assert(!valid_length(65));\n}\n/* Run in a host harness with assertions enabled, not NDEBUG. */',
 ['Compile the pure validator on the host and call test_lengths from a harness main.','Write tests for a complete packet parser including output state.','Run a target integration test through the actual UART path.','Record why host tests do not validate clocks, pin mux or DMA configuration.'],
 'Each test has a specific oracle; rejected packets preserve prior application state.',
 'Build a test matrix for empty, minimum, maximum, oversized, truncated and invalid-checksum frames.',
 'What belongs in host tests versus hardware-in-loop tests?',
 'Pure logic and deterministic boundary cases fit fast host tests. Register integration, interrupts, electrical timing, DMA/cache behavior and reset recovery require target or hardware-in-loop evidence.');
lesson(4,'Controlled Fault Injection',
 'Fault injection deliberately violates a chosen assumption to observe detection and recovery. Limit scope, define the expected response and make reset/recovery possible. Inject at interfaces so that results are repeatable. A random crash is not a controlled experiment.',
 'A sensor-read wrapper returns a timeout on every tenth call. The application should preserve its last valid reading, mark it stale and recover on the next successful sample. This tests recovery without misconfiguring the physical bus.',
 '#include <stdbool.h>\n#include <stdint.h>\nbool read_sensor_stub(uint16_t *value) {\n    static uint32_t calls;\n    if (!value) return false;\n    if (++calls % 10U == 0U) return false;\n    *value = 250U;\n    return true;\n}',
 ['Define the stale-data indicator, timeout count and recovery acceptance criteria.','Use the stub behind the same application interface as the real sensor.','Run 100 calls and inspect ten injected failures.','Remove the injection configuration and verify it is excluded from the production build.'],
 'Exactly ten simulated failures occur in 100 non-null calls; the application recovers without treating stale data as fresh.',
 'Add stuck input, delayed completion and invalid checksum injections. For each, define the expected fault counter and recovery state.',
 'How do you prevent test hooks from becoming production defects?',
 'Keep injection explicit and disabled by default, review build configuration, test production artifacts separately, and make injection status visible in test evidence. Avoid hidden runtime triggers.');
lesson(4,'Regression Testing After a Correction',
 'A regression suite protects the corrected invariant and nearby behavior. First demonstrate a test failing against the defective implementation; then run it against the correction. Test the production build as well as the diagnostic build. Repeat tests across relevant boards rather than assuming one MCU validates another.',
 'A buffer fix rejects oversized packets. Recheck maximum legal packets, repeated errors, recovery, counters and throughput. A fix that avoids corruption by discarding every packet fails the functional requirement.',
 '# Suggested reproducible sequence (adapt commands to your build):\n# 1. Check out defect revision in an isolated checkout.\n# 2. Run reproducer; save expected failing result.\n# 3. Check out correction; run same reproducer.\n# 4. Run boundary + recovery + throughput suite.\n# 5. Flash release ELF; record target/build ID and results.',
 ['Assign a stable ID to the original reproducer.','Keep stimulus identical before and after the correction.','Run adjacent boundary and long-run recovery cases.','Record build ID, board, elapsed time, attempts and failures in the regression report.'],
 'The original reproducer changes from fail to pass while normal operation remains correct.',
 'Define a nightly hardware test for 10,000 packets with a deterministic seed, loss count and reset-reason capture.',
 'Is one successful reproduction test enough to close an intermittent bug?',
 'Usually not. Match the original failure conditions and rate, increase coverage and exposure, and report the residual uncertainty. Explain why the corrected mechanism prevents the failure rather than relying only on a clean run.');
lesson(4,'Professional Firmware Debugging Reports',
 'A debugging report lets another engineer reproduce and assess the conclusion. Lead with the user-visible failure and result, then provide environment, trigger, observations, root cause, correction and validation. Label inferences clearly. Attach exact build artifacts and separate remaining risks from verified results.',
 'A report for UART loss should state the failing traffic rate, board, buffer size, lost sequence count, actual overwrite mechanism and the correction’s measured throughput. “Changed DMA code, works now” cannot be independently reviewed.',
 'Issue: UART packets lost at a defined burst rate\nEnvironment: MCU / board revision / probe / commit / ELF hash\nReproducer: stimulus, reset mode, repetitions\nEvidence: sequence gaps, counters, trace, register snapshot\nMechanism: violated invariant and causal chain\nCorrection: changed ownership/bounds logic\nValidation: before/after + boundaries + recovery + load\nLimits: untested boards and remaining uncertainty',
 ['Use the report editor below to document one completed lab.','Include at least one measurement and one observation that rejects a competing hypothesis.','Link the exact ELF/map and test stimulus in your repository.','Ask another engineer to reproduce using only your report.'],
 'A reviewer can repeat the experiment and understand both the correction and its limits.',
 'Complete the UART recorder capstone and publish a concise case study with original evidence and a regression matrix.',
 'How would you explain a complex firmware bug in an interview?',
 'Describe the symptom, constraints, competing hypotheses, discriminating experiment, causal mechanism and validated fix. Quantify the outcome and explain an alternative you rejected based on evidence.');
rtosLesson(0,'FreeRTOS Task Scheduling and Context Switching',
 'FreeRTOS schedules tasks by priority and a ready list, then performs a context switch when the current task blocks, yields or is preempted. A task control block holds the stack pointer, state, priority and event information. The scheduler is not “magic”: it selects the next runnable task using a deterministic policy, then restores the saved context of that task.',
 'A sensor task and a telemetry task are both ready. The sensor task runs first because it has higher priority, but once it blocks waiting for a mailbox it hands control to the telemetry task. A short task that calls vTaskDelay can still yield to a lower-priority task if the system is configured for preemption and the current task has reached a ready state transition.',
 '#include "FreeRTOS.h"\n#include "task.h"\nstatic void vSensorTask(void *pvParameters) {\n    for (;;) {\n        /* Read ADC, publish sample */\n        vTaskDelay(pdMS_TO_TICKS(50));\n    }\n}\nstatic void vTelemetryTask(void *pvParameters) {\n    for (;;) {\n        /* Send queue data */\n        taskYIELD();\n    }\n}\nvoid vAppStart(void) {\n    xTaskCreate(vSensorTask, "SENSOR", 256, NULL, 3, NULL);\n    xTaskCreate(vTelemetryTask, "TEL", 256, NULL, 2, NULL);\n}',
 ['Trace the priority values and expected scheduling order.','Explain why taskYIELD changes the current task state without necessarily blocking.','Identify the state transition for a task that calls vTaskDelay.','Predict the behavior when the high-priority sensor task remains runnable and never blocks.'],
 'The higher-priority task keeps the CPU until it blocks or yields; a ready lower-priority task cannot run while the higher-priority task remains runnable.',
 'Write a tiny trace showing READY, RUNNING, BLOCKED and SUSPENDED transitions for a two-task priority system.','What is the real cost of a context switch?',
 'A context switch costs CPU time and stack movement, but it is often cheaper than blocking or busy-waiting. The real question is whether the system is making forward progress under its timing constraints and whether the saved state and stack remain valid under interrupts and task deletions.');
rtosLesson(1,'Priority, Preemption and Priority Inversion',
 'Priority inversion happens when a low-priority task holds a resource needed by a higher-priority task, while an unrelated medium-priority task keeps preempting the low-priority task. FreeRTOS supports mutexes and priority inheritance to bound this phenomenon. Correct task priorities are a design contract, not a vague preference.',
 'A motor control task holds a mutex while sending a command, while a telemetry task with higher priority waits. A logger task with medium priority keeps preempting, so the motor control task cannot finish, delaying the high-priority work. Priority inheritance or a better decomposition is needed.',
 '#include "FreeRTOS.h"\n#include "semphr.h"\nstatic SemaphoreHandle_t xMotorMutex;\nstatic void vMotorTask(void *pv) {\n    for (;;) {\n        xSemaphoreTake(xMotorMutex, portMAX_DELAY);\n        /* drive output */\n        xSemaphoreGive(xMotorMutex);\n        vTaskDelay(pdMS_TO_TICKS(10));\n    }\n}\nstatic void vLoggerTask(void *pv) {\n    for (;;) {\n        vTaskDelay(pdMS_TO_TICKS(5));\n        /* medium priority work */\n    }\n}',
 ['State which task is blocked and which tasks are ready.','Decide whether the mutex is the correct primitive for the use case.','Model how priority inheritance changes the ordering.','Identify a corner case where a mutex is not enough because the task holds it too long or the same resource is used across unrelated services.'],
 'Priority inversion causes unbounded or excessive blocking. A mutex plus priority inheritance reduces the worst-case delay by making the lock owner inherit the highest waiting priority.',
 'Design a policy for a shared SPI driver with one low-priority writer and several higher-priority readers. Explain the priority model and the lock duration.','Why is a “high priority task always preempts” assumption dangerous?',
 'It ignores resource ownership, interrupt latency and lock duration. In real systems, the highest-priority task can still be delayed when it waits on a resource held by a lower-priority task, or when a long critical section or ISR blocks progress. Hardware and software design contracts must cover that.' );
rtosLesson(2,'Queues, Semaphores and ISR Safety',
 'FreeRTOS communication primitives are explicit contracts about ownership, waiting, and wakeup. Queues move data between tasks and ISR contexts. Semaphores represent availability or events, while mutexes represent exclusive ownership. The main design question is whether the producer is blocked, the consumer is waiting, or the ISR must complete quickly.',
 'A UART RX ISR writes bytes into a queue. A protocol parser task drains them. An overloaded queue should drop new data, count the loss, and keep the system alive rather than deadlocking or overflowing a static buffer. In ISR code, use xQueueSendFromISR instead of blocking calls.',
 '#include "FreeRTOS.h"\n#include "queue.h"\nstatic QueueHandle_t xRxQueue;\nvoid vUartIsr(void) {\n    uint8_t byte = UART_ReadByte();\n    BaseType_t xHigherPriorityTaskWoken = pdFALSE;\n    xQueueSendFromISR(xRxQueue, &byte, &xHigherPriorityTaskWoken);\n    portYIELD_FROM_ISR(xHigherPriorityTaskWoken);\n}',
 ['Compare queue depth, ring buffer size and memory allocation choices.','Describe the difference between a binary semaphore and a counting semaphore.','Design a bounded recovery when the queue gets full.','List a correct ISR-safe pattern and an incorrect one.'],
 'The ISR writes to a queue without blocking, and the consumer task processes the frame as soon as the queue has enough data. If the queue fills, the app counts drops and returns to a safe state.',
 'Create a queue-based design for a sensor acquisition pipeline with three stages: acquisition, validation and logging. Name the boundary of ownership and the queue depth policy.','What is the common bug behind “the queue looked full but the task never woke up”?',
 'The queue full condition, ISR wake-up, and task wait state were not correctly coordinated. The producer may have dropped data, the consumer may not have re-enabled a higher-priority wakeup, or a queue length change may have invalidated the original design assumptions. Verify the actual wake-up path and the data ownership protocol.' );
rtosLesson(3,'Memory, Stack and Static Allocation in RTOS',
 'FreeRTOS task stacks and heap choices directly affect timing, determinism and crash behavior. Static allocation is easier to audit and predict; dynamic allocation with malloc-like behavior can be convenient but adds fragmentation, timing variability and memory exhaustion risk. A task stack overflow may corrupt the TCB or the scheduler state itself.',
 'A network task with a 256-byte stack silently corrupts memory during a large string parse. The fix is to increase stack depth, move parser state to static memory, and validate high-watermark usage under stress. Stack overflow often hides behind an apparently unrelated crash.',
 '#include "FreeRTOS.h"\n#include "task.h"\nstatic StackType_t xTaskStack[512];\nstatic StaticTask_t xTaskBuffer;\nstatic void vParserTask(void *pv) {\n    for (;;) {\n        /* parse packet */\n    }\n}\nvoid vCreateParserTask(void) {\n    xTaskCreateStatic(vParserTask, "PARSER", 512, NULL, 2, xTaskStack, &xTaskBuffer);\n}',
 ['Explain when static allocation is preferable to heap allocation.','Inspect a stack-high-water-mark example using uxTaskGetStackHighWaterMark.','Decide whether message buffers should be on the heap, static memory or a fixed-size pool.','Describe why a memory leak is especially dangerous in a long-running RTOS system.'],
 'Static allocation makes object lifetime and stack size explicit. High-water-mark reporting shows whether the chosen stack size is adequate under stress.',
 'Design a safe memory strategy for a device driver that queues packets and logs faults. State which objects are static, which are dynamic and why.','Why is “it only crashes under load” a classic RTOS bug pattern?',
 'It usually means a stack, queue or heap boundary is being exceeded under a specific timing pattern. The bug is latent until allocation, priority inversion, ISR timing or buffer depth pushes the system past the invariant boundary. Treat it as a resource-contract problem, not a random failure.' );
rtosLesson(4,'Task Notifications, Timers and Low-Power Idle',
 'Task notifications and software timers are efficient ways to signal events without creating extra queues or semaphores. Tickless idle reduces power consumption by stopping the periodic tick when all tasks are idle. The corner case is not just “idle is low power”; it is “what happens at the next wake-up event and how do we maintain time correctness?”',
 'A wireless module wakes the system on an external interrupt, sends a packet, and then returns to sleep. The system must reliably distinguish between a stale event and a new edge while keeping RTC/time tracking accurate. A tickless design can optimize power only when all scheduling edges are correctly considered.',
 '#include "FreeRTOS.h"\n#include "task.h"\nstatic void vRadioTask(void *pv) {\n    uint32_t ulEvent;\n    for (;;) {\n        ulEvent = ulTaskNotifyTake(pdTRUE, portMAX_DELAY);\n        if (ulEvent) {\n            /* handle radio packet */\n        }\n    }\n}\nstatic void vWakeFromIsr(void) {\n    BaseType_t xHigherPriorityTaskWoken = pdFALSE;\n    vTaskNotifyGiveFromISR(xTaskGetCurrentTaskHandle(), &xHigherPriorityTaskWoken);\n    portYIELD_FROM_ISR(xHigherPriorityTaskWoken);\n}',
 ['Explain when a task notification is better than a queue.','Compare a timer callback to a dedicated timer task.','Identify the conditions under which tickless idle is safe.','Describe a re-entrancy risk when reusing a task notification across multiple interrupts.'],
 'A task notification is a lightweight one-to-one event that avoids queue management overhead; a timer task or queue remains useful when you need message ordering, payloads, or multiple readers.',
 'Write a design note for an energy-optimized sensor node that wakes on GPIO edge, reads a sample, and sleeps until the next scheduled event. Include the assumptions for tickless idle and the recovery path for missed wakeups.','How do you validate that a low-power design is actually safe?',
 'Measure deadlines, reset timing, wake-up latency, and notification ordering under both normal and noisy conditions. A low-power mode is not “safe” until its timing and wakeup invariants are proven with the actual hardware and the real interrupt sources.' );
const projects = [
 {id:'flight-recorder',title:'Build a firmware flight recorder',tag:'CAPSTONE · F401 + H7',summary:'Turn an intermittent UART failure into a reproducible engineering case study.',parts:['STM32 board and ST-LINK','USB UART adapter with 3.3 V logic, optional logic analyzer','Loopback wire or second UART transmitter'],steps:['Generate a UART + timer project for your exact board. Transmit numbered, checksummed frames from a host script or second board.','Implement a bounded ring buffer with explicit producer/consumer ownership and overflow counters. Start with interrupt RX on F401; extend to DMA after correctness is measured.','Record timestamp, sequence, event type and error flags in a fixed-size RAM event ring. Define overwrite-oldest versus drop-new behavior.','Inject bursts and delayed parsing. Save the last events, UART status and reset reason when failure is detected.','Correct the first demonstrated invariant violation. On H743, add a verified DMA memory placement/cache handoff design.','Deliver the source, matching ELF/map, wiring notes, 10,000-frame regression results and a one-page incident report.'],accept:'No undetected sequence loss; every intentional overload increments a counter. Recovery succeeds after malformed traffic. Record measured throughput and maximum observed latency.'},
 {id:'crash-capture',title:'Create a crash investigation kit',tag:'FAULT ANALYSIS · INTERMEDIATE',summary:'Capture fault evidence and resolve a crash address back to its instruction.',parts:['F401RE or Cortex-M7 H743 board','ST-LINK and STM32CubeIDE','Matching ELF, map and compiler toolchain'],steps:['Complete the controlled UDF lab and save SCB status plus handler LR.','Validate the chosen stack address and frame type before interpreting the stacked registers.','Design a bounded crash record with version, build ID, raw status, validity flags and checksum. Avoid printf and allocation in the handler.','If persisting across reset, reserve an appropriate no-init or retained memory area and verify startup/reset behavior for your part.','Decode the saved PC offline with addr2line and inspect the matching disassembly.','Test ordinary reset, deliberate fault, corrupt-record checksum and missing ELF cases.'],accept:'A deterministic injected fault resolves to the matching source instruction. Invalid or unavailable frames are explicitly reported; a corrupt crash record is rejected.'},
 {id:'timing-bench',title:'Measure a real-time deadline',tag:'TIMING · F401 + H7',summary:'Compare cycle counts and GPIO traces under realistic interrupt load.',parts:['STM32 board','Logic analyzer or oscilloscope','A spare GPIO selected from the board schematic'],steps:['Configure a periodic work item with a documented deadline. Use a known bounded workload and retain its output so optimization cannot remove it.','Pulse a GPIO around the workload and independently record CYCCNT deltas.','Measure the empty instrumentation baseline and validate actual core frequency.','Repeat under increasing interrupt load and record maximum observed latency and deadline misses.','For cached M7 devices, compare documented warm and cold conditions and memory placement.','Publish raw measurements, histograms, configuration and a discussion of why observed maxima are not formal worst-case bounds.'],accept:'Measurements from the two methods agree within documented overhead; every missed deadline is counted and test conditions are reproducible.'}
];
const posts = [
 {id:'volatile',title:'The volatile trap: visibility is only the beginning',tag:'C & CONCURRENCY',summary:'Why an ISR counter still loses events after you add volatile.',body:['Imagine main reads events=3. An ISR increments it to 4. Main clears it to 0. Four produced events have been represented as only three processed events. Each individual access may be valid, yet the read-and-clear protocol is broken.','volatile prevents the compiler from treating the object as an ordinary unchanging local value. It does not make a sequence of accesses indivisible, lock another core, or make DMA coherent with cache.','On a single Cortex-M core, a short critical section that preserves the previous interrupt mask can protect a snapshot-and-clear operation from a maskable ISR. It cannot exclude DMA or another core. Use a protocol appropriate to the actual competing agents.','Study Shared Variables and Race Conditions, then verify the invariant produced = processed + pending under increasing load. Include overflow in your design, otherwise a sufficiently long run invalidates the arithmetic.']},
 {id:'h7-cache',title:'The bytes arrived. Why does the H7 see old data?',tag:'HARDWARE NOTES',summary:'A practical way to reason about DMA, cache lines and ownership.',body:['A DMA controller writes RAM directly. A Cortex-M7 CPU can read its cached copy of that memory instead. Transfer completion proves the DMA finished its work, not that the CPU cache now contains those bytes.','Before changing cache settings, verify the DMA controller can reach the chosen memory region and that transfer counters advance. STM32H7 is a family; use the exact part reference manual rather than assuming every RAM bank is accessible.','A dedicated full-cache-line-aligned RX buffer avoids sharing lines with unrelated data. Prepare the buffer before DMA ownership, keep CPU writes out during transfer, and invalidate after confirmed completion before CPU consumption. A documented non-cacheable MPU region is another design option.','Do not invalidate arbitrary unaligned memory and hope for correctness. Dirty neighboring CPU data can be lost. Use the UART/DMA lesson and ST AN4839 to define the complete ownership protocol.']},
 {id:'debug-delay',title:'When adding a delay “fixes” your firmware',tag:'DEBUGGING PRACTICE',summary:'Treat disappearing symptoms as an experiment result, then keep investigating.',body:['A delay may let a DMA transfer complete, reduce traffic enough to avoid overflow, or change the relative timing of two writers. Those mechanisms require different corrections. The delay alone does not distinguish them.','Write a prediction for each explanation. If the producer outruns the consumer, buffer occupancy should trend upward. If completion is missing, the CPU should read data while the peripheral still owns the buffer. Measure those predictions with bounded counters or GPIO trace.','Once you identify the violated invariant, replace accidental timing with a completion event, capacity check or ownership handoff. Keep the original stimulus as a regression test.','In an interview, explain how you separated correlation from mechanism. The strongest story is an experiment another engineer can repeat, not a list of settings you changed.']}
];
